xt": "$-10(100 x+y+z)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc85"}, {"id": "854537", "text": "$10(100 x+y+z)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc86"}, {"id": "02afd6", "text": "$x-10 y-10 z$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc87"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc88", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df8c"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8808"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87ea"], "question": {"text": "The reaction\n\n$\\mathrm{MgO}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g}),$ for which\n\n$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\circ}=+491.1 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}^{\\circ}=198.0 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$\n\nis not feasible at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$. Temperature above which reaction will be feasible is"}, "options": [{"id": "9b3488", "text": "$2040.5 \\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc89"}, {"id": "088f27", "text": "$1890.0 \\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc8a"}, {"id": "a94f81", "text": "$2480.3 \\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc8b"}, {"id": "ad29ab", "text": "$2380.5 \\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc8c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc8d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df8c"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4783"], "concepts": ["6162fb8c6ed13040ec8502c6"], "question": {"text": "$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{S}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\frac{3}{2} \\mathrm{O}_2(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SO}_3(\\mathrm{~g})+2 x \\mathrm{kcal} \\\\\n& \\mathrm{SO}_2(\\mathrm{~g})+\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_2(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SO}_3(\\mathrm{~g})+y \\mathrm{kcal}\n\\end{aligned}$\n\nThe heat of formation of $\\mathrm{SO}_2(\\mathrm{~g})$ is given by :"}, "options": [{"id": "2f5a00", "text": "$x+y \\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc8e"}, {"id": "3db505", "text": "$y-2 x \\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc8f"}, {"id": "532d54", "text": "$\\frac{2 x}{y} \\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc90"}, {"id": "47f3d7", "text": "$2 x+y \\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc91"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc92", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df8c"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b476e"], "concepts": ["6162fb896ed13040ec850101"], "question": {"text": "For a diatomic ideal gas in a closed system, which of the following plots does not correctly describe the relation between various thermodynamic quantities?"}, "options": [{"id": "50ada5", "text": "<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F6ef80d94-9903-48c6-af90-5b8684425078-be68610b-ba7b-4720-9896-39fc498b97ea-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 178px; height: 168px;\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc93"}, {"id": "0a0e71", "text": "<p><img height=\"179\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F91fe90ed-562c-49be-873c-2462238a216b-image.png?alt=media\" width=\"188\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc94"}, {"id": "400337", "text": "<p><img height=\"168\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F75b526fd-80e8-407a-8307-1a51c4c6a22a-image.png?alt=media\" width=\"177\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc95"}, {"id": "b5de12", "text": "<p><img height=\"171\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F0afdaad4-2e64-44fb-8f54-79f95e03ee74-image.png?alt=media\" width=\"169\" /></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc96"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc97", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df8c"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4783"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b473a"], "question": {"text": "Consider the given data :<br />(a) $\\mathrm{HCl}(\\mathrm{g})+10 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HCl}.10 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}$<br />$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-69.01 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$<br />(b) $\\mathrm{HCl}(\\mathrm{g})+40 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HCl}.40 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}$<br />$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-72.79 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$<br />Choose the correct statement :"}, "options": [{"id": "0cf920", "text": "Dissolution of gas in water is an endothermic process", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc98"}, {"id": "09a17b", "text": "The heat of solution depends on the amount of solvent.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc99"}, {"id": "aaaca7", "text": "The heat of dilution for the $\\mathrm{HCl}\\left(\\mathrm{HCl} \\cdot 10 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}\\right.$ to $\\left.\\mathrm{HCl}.40 \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}\\right)$ is $3.78 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc9a"}, {"id": "ec2a01", "text": "The heat of formation of HCl solution is represented by both (a) and (b)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bc9b"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:47.797Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:47.797Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bce2"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bce0", "title": "Chemistry Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d91", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bcc4", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df8c"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b476e"], "concepts": ["6162fb896ed13040ec850107"], "question": {"text": "Three moles of an ideal gas are compressed isothermally from \\(60 \\mathrm{~L}\\) to \\(20 \\mathrm{~L}\\) using constant pressure of \\(5 \\mathrm{~atm}\\). Heat exchange \\(\\mathrm{Q}\\) for the compression is - ______ Lit. atm."}, "options": [{"id": "2fa4bc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bcc5"}, {"id": "9a6e8c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bcc6"}, {"id": "1ca02a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bcc7"}, {"id": "6c7305", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bcc8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f97e3e37d7ebba3bcc9", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0df8c"], "topics": ["60e5671c499d1e49588f8808"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87ea"], "question": {"text": "<p>Consider the graph of Gibbs free energy G vs extent of reaction. The number of statement/s from the following which are true with respect to points (a), (b) and (c) is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi></math></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fa341f6d3-c5af-47b4-aa35-e1bbdd5f45f6-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 202px;\" /></p><p>A. Reaction is spontaneous at (a) and (b)</p><p>B. Reaction is at equilibrium at point (b) and non-spontaneous at point (c)</p><p>C. Reaction is spontaneous at (a) and non-spontaneous at (c)</p><p>D. Reaction is non-spontaneous at (a) and (b)</p>"}, "options"